msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:24
msgid ""
"The two-segment form is ambiguous on its face — `a:b` could be url+name "
"or path+name — and the scheme resolves it. The last two forms **require**"
" the scheme: without it, a missing URL would otherwise become a value "
"assembled from nothing, and it is an error instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:28
msgid ""
"Note the trailing colon in `<url>:<path>:`. It is what distinguishes that"
" form from `<url>:<name>`, and it is not optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:31
msgid ""
"Zero segments, or four and more, is an error naming the count. An empty "
"string parses into a specifier with nothing set — not an error at parse "
"time, only when it is validated as an actual input."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:35
msgid "Colons inside a value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:37
msgid ""
"Wrap the segment in quotes. A URL port, a Windows drive letter, a name "
"containing a colon:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:43
msgid ""
"A quote only opens a quoted region at the **start** of a segment; "
"anywhere else it stays a literal character. The whole specifier may also "
"be wrapped in quotes, and the scheme is still detected underneath."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:47
msgid "Several documents at once"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:49
msgid "On the command line, repeat `-i` or give several values after one:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:55
msgid ""
"Several documents in one invocation are **one job over a bundle**, not a "
"loop of separate runs. That distinction is the reason the feature exists,"
" and it matters most when the files are pieces of *one* report: "
"publishers routinely split a report across files aimed at different "
"audiences, so the holdings are in one file and the managers of those "
"funds in another, and neither file on its own contains enough to extract "
"the holdings with their managers attached."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:61
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Passed together, the pages of every file are classified per file and then"
" **scheduled as one pool**, so a step reading one document produces the "
"filter data a later step over another document is given. The bundle "
"yields what no member of it yields alone. "
"{doc}`../../reference/design/multidocument` is the full account."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:66
msgid ""
"From the environment and from a batch cell, where there is only one "
"string to work with, the specifiers are separated by a **pipe**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:73
msgid "Directories"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:75
msgid ""
"A path that is a directory **expands to every PDF in it**, and each "
"expanded document is named `<name>/<file name>` — so one specifier can "
"name a whole folder and still produce distinguishable rows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:83
msgid "gives documents called `2024/eurizon.pdf`, `2024/amundi.pdf`, and so on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:85
msgid "The name is not decoration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:87
msgid ""
"It is the value that appears in the **`Report` column** of every output "
"table, and it is how rows from two documents are told apart afterwards. "
"Left to default, it is the whole absolute path — which works, and is "
"unpleasant to read in a spreadsheet, and will bite you the first time you"
" use `--separate-out`, since the name becomes part of the file name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:92
msgid "Downloading, and `save_pdf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/user/documents.md:94
msgid ""
"Downloaded documents are **kept** by default. `--no-download` does not "
"suppress the download; it suppresses keeping the file. The behaviour is a"
" small matrix, and it is worth reading because the two halves genuinely "
"differ in intent — with saving on, a path is *where to put it*; with "
"saving off, a path is *where to look first*."
msgstr ""

